\title{Binary Tower Fields}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    The tower
    #{\mathbb{F}_2 \subset \mathbb{F}_{2^2} \subset \mathbb{F}_{2^4} \subset \cdots \subset \mathbb{F}_{2^{128}}},
    built by iterated quadratic extension, and the arithmetic that makes it attractive. This is the
    ground [Binius](dhsorens-003J) stands on.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{The tower construction, and why every subfield embedding is free.}
        \li{Why binary: no modular reduction, characteristic 2 arithmetic maps onto bit operations,
        and small values cost small fields.}
        \li{The claimed cost comparison against Mersenne31, and under what measure it holds.}
        \li{Multilinear polynomials over a tower, and why the multilinear setting rather than the
        univariate one.}
        \li{[Ring switching](dhsorens-0035): moving between tower levels inside a single argument.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [Binary tower
    fields](https://www.irreducible.com/posts/binary-tower-fields-are-the-future-of-verifiable-computing)
    (Irreducible), [SNARKs on binary
    fields](https://blog.lambdaclass.com/snarks-on-binary-fields-binius/) (LambdaClass), [The
    Small-Field Turn](https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1371).
}
